<resources>
    <string name="app_name">SlowLock</string>
    <!-- The app list's title. New in feature 004: the screen previously had no heading. -->
    <string name="app_list_title">Choose an app</string>
    <string name="app_list_search_hint">Search apps</string>
    <string name="app_list_clear_query">Clear search</string>
    <string name="app_list_empty">No apps to show.</string>
    <string name="app_list_no_results">No apps match \"%1$s\".</string>
    <string name="app_list_unavailable">That app is no longer available.</string>
    <string name="app_list_icon_description">%1$s icon</string>

    <!--
        The back affordance's label, shared by every screen that has one (contract U1).

        Feature 004 gave the three configuration screens a single ScreenHeader, so the
        per-screen delay_config_back and shortcut_config_back strings it replaces are now
        unused. They are left in place rather than deleted: FR-042 confines this feature to
        display values, and removing resource names is a separate, riskier change.
    -->
    <string name="back">Back</string>

    <!--
        The entire text of the wait screen (FR-025, obligations W8 and W11).

        It must not name the target app, must not mention a duration, and must not suggest that
        anything is loading. "Please wait" is deliberately the dullest phrasing available: the
        screen's job is to be uninteresting for as long as the delay lasts, and a friendlier or
        more explanatory line is a line worth reading twice.

        Feature 004 lower-cased it to match the design source. Stored lower-case rather than
        transformed at display time (contract C8): a locale-sensitive case transform on
        user-visible text is a trap, and the string resource should hold exactly what is drawn.
    -->
    <string name="wait_message">please wait</string>

    <string name="delay_config_title">Wait before opening</string>
    <string name="delay_config_back">Back</string>
    <!--
        The primary action, reworded by feature 004 (FR-020).

        "Next" named the mechanism; the design names the outcome. The user is going to choose an
        icon, and saying so is what lets the step indicator the canvas draws stay out of Phase 1
        without the flow feeling unmoored.
    -->
    <string name="delay_config_next">Choose the icon</string>

    <!--
        The unit caption beneath the delay numeral (FR-014).

        Stored capitalised on purpose (contract C8): the capitalisation is stylistic, and running
        uppercase() on user-visible text at display time is a locale trap - Turkish dotted and
        dotless i being the standing example. Letter-spacing comes from the text style, never
        from spaces inserted between the letters, which would destroy it for screen readers.

        Translators: render this in your language's normal wording; capitalise it if your script
        has case, and simply do not if it does not.
    -->
    <string name="delay_config_seconds_caption">SECONDS</string>

    <!-- The slider's range endpoints. Mono, and deliberately terse. -->
    <string name="delay_config_range_min">1s</string>
    <string name="delay_config_range_max">30s</string>

    <!--
        A one-tap delay preset's visible label (FR-017). %1$d is the number of seconds.

        Its visible text is a bare value, so it needs the separate description below to announce
        as the action it performs rather than as two characters (FR-044).

        The description is a plain string rather than a plural because no preset is 1 second -
        they are 5, 10 and 30 (DelayRange.PRESETS, asserted by DelayRangeTest). If a preset of 1
        is ever added, this becomes a <plurals> in the same commit.
    -->
    <string name="delay_config_preset">%1$ds</string>
    <string name="delay_config_preset_description">Wait %1$d seconds</string>

    <!--
        The readout beside the slider (FR-007, obligation D4).

        A <plurals> rather than "%1$d seconds", and load-bearing rather than defensive: the
        slider's minimum is one second, so "1 second" is a value the user can actually select and
        "1 seconds" is a bug one drag away. It was written as a plural while the minimum was
        still five, on the argument that the range was provisional — which is exactly what it
        then turned out to be. A language whose plural rules differ from English's has forms this
        app never chose; that part remains a translator's to see, not a JVM test's.
    -->
    <plurals name="delay_seconds">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d second</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d seconds</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
        Feature 004 made "lock" the canonical user-facing noun (FR-041). "Shortcut" now refers
        only to the Android mechanism and survives in places the user never reads - resource
        names, class names, contract filenames, and the launcher's own confirmation dialog.

        The resource NAMES below are deliberately untouched (FR-042). ShortcutLaunchActivity's
        fully-qualified name is written into every icon already sitting on a user's home screen,
        and renaming anything in that chain to match a wording change would break them. The word
        the user reads and the identifier the system stores are allowed to differ; here they must.
    -->
    <string name="shortcut_config_title">New lock</string>

    <!--
        The preview card's delay line (FR-022). "10 second wait".

        Both English forms are identical on purpose: "second" is an adjective here, as in "a
        10-second wait", so it does not inflect. The design source does the same.

        It is still a <plurals> rather than a format string, because that identity is a fact about
        English and not about every language. A translator into a language that does inflect an
        attributive numeral has the forms available; with a plain string they would not.

        Deliberately not a reuse of delay_seconds: that one reads as a bare quantity ("10
        seconds") and this one as a description of the lock ("10 second wait").
    -->
    <plurals name="delay_wait">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d second wait</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d second wait</item>
    </plurals>
    <string name="shortcut_config_back">Back</string>
    <string name="shortcut_config_create">Add to home screen</string>
    <!--
        The section label above the icon-treatment tiles (FR-023).

        Stored capitalised because the capitalisation is stylistic (contract C8) - see the note on
        delay_config_seconds_caption for why it is not uppercased at display time.
    -->
    <string name="shortcut_config_icon_label">ICON</string>

    <!--
        The line beneath "Add to home screen" (FR-025).

        It sets the expectation that the next thing the user sees is a system dialog they did not
        ask SlowLock for. The app cannot suppress it and should not pretend otherwise.
    -->
    <string name="shortcut_config_confirm_note">Your launcher will ask you to confirm.</string>

    <string name="shortcut_treatment_original">Original</string>
    <string name="shortcut_treatment_invert">Inverted</string>
    <string name="shortcut_treatment_gray">Gray</string>
    <string name="shortcut_target_unavailable">That app is no longer available, so no lock was created.</string>
    <string name="shortcut_launch_unavailable">That app is no longer available.</string>
    <string name="shortcut_icon_unavailable">That app\'s icon couldn\'t be loaded, so no lock can be created right now. Go back and try again.</string>
    <!--
        The heading above the unsupported-launcher explanation (FR-034).

        Stored capitalised because the capitalisation is stylistic (contract C8) - see the note on
        delay_config_seconds_caption for why it is not uppercased at display time.

        It names the consequence the user can see ("no room on the home screen") rather than the
        mechanism they cannot act on. The sentence beneath it is what explains and what to do.
    -->
    <string name="pin_unsupported_eyebrow">NO ROOM ON THE HOME SCREEN</string>

    <string name="pin_unsupported_message">Your home screen won\'t let SlowLock add icons to it. Switching to a different home screen app should fix this.</string>
    <string name="pin_unsupported_open_settings">Choose home screen app</string>
    <string name="pin_unsupported_recheck">Check again</string>
    <string name="pin_unsupported_settings_failed">That setting couldn\'t be opened on this device. You can change your home screen app from your phone\'s settings.</string>

    <!--
        The intro screen (005, FR-018) - what a fresh install opens on, and the only place the app
        explains itself.

        Stored capitalised because the capitalisation is stylistic (contract C8) - see the note on
        delay_config_seconds_caption for why it is not uppercased at display time.
    -->
    <string name="intro_eyebrow">A PAUSE, NOT A BLOCK</string>

    <string name="intro_title">Put a pause in front of an app</string>

    <!--
        FR-018, and a product commitment rather than copy: the second sentence is the app promising
        what it will *not* do. Nothing is blocked - the app always opens - and nothing is counted:
        statistics of any kind are permanently out of scope. Translations must keep both halves.
    -->
    <string name="intro_body">SlowLock puts a short wait in front of an app you open too easily. You choose the app and how long to wait, and SlowLock adds an icon to your home screen that waits before opening it.\n\nNothing is blocked, and nothing is counted. The app always opens, and SlowLock never tracks how often you use it.</string>

    <string name="intro_start">Set up a lock</string>
    <!--
        The Locks screen (005, FR-010) - what a returning user opens on.
    -->
    <!--
        "Locks", not "Your locks", since 007 FR-007. The word is what the `New · Locks` artboard
        draws, and at the redesigned title's size the possessive was doing no work: this screen is
        only ever reached by the person whose locks they are.
    -->
    <string name="locks_title">Locks</string>

    <!--
        The count beneath the title (FR-011).

        It states the number and NOTHING else. The design source reads "3 ON YOUR HOME SCREEN";
        that half is deliberately not shipped, because Android cannot tell the app whether those
        icons are still there and asserting it is exactly the claim Constitution I forbids. A user
        who deleted an icon would be told it was still on their home screen.

        A <plurals> rather than "%1$d locks": one lock is the commonest state this screen has, and
        "1 locks" is one tap away from being read by every new user.
    -->
    <plurals name="locks_count">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d lock</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d locks</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
        The SAME count, capitalised, as the Locks screen draws it (007 FR-008, contract L4).

        A second resource rather than uppercase() at display time, because contract C8 forbids that
        transform on user-visible text - see the note on delay_config_seconds_caption for the
        Turkish dotted/dotless i trap it avoids. locks_count above is what a screen reader is given,
        so TalkBack hears "3 locks" and is never handed capitals to spell out (007 FR-012).

        Translators: capitalise this in your language only if your script has case and the
        convention makes sense; otherwise render it exactly as locks_count. The letter-spacing the
        design shows comes from the text style, never from spaces between the letters, which would
        destroy the string for screen readers.
    -->
    <plurals name="locks_count_caption">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d LOCK</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d LOCKS</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
        The screen's single action (FR-014). Stored with its "+" rather than composing one at
        display time, so a translator can move or drop it.
    -->
    <string name="locks_new">+ New lock</string>

    <!--
        The delay badge at the trailing edge of a Locks row (007 FR-015).

        %1$d is the delay in seconds. Translatable, because "s" is not a universal abbreviation for
        a second and some scripts put the unit on the other side of the number.

        THIS IS THE FORM FOR THE EYE ONLY. It is deliberately terse, and a screen reader must never
        be left with it - "10s" is read as "ten s". LocksScreen gives the badge a
        contentDescription holding the delay_wait plural instead, so the row is spoken as
        "10 second wait" (007 FR-018, contract L7).
    -->
    <string name="locks_delay_badge">%1$ds</string>

    <string name="locks_icon_description">%1$s icon</string>

    <!--
        The unavailable row (FR-020, contract K3).

        The app is gone but the lock is not, and this row says so with the only thing left to
        identify it by: %1$s is the package name. It must not suggest the lock was removed - it
        was not, and the icon on the home screen may still be sitting there waiting. Only the user
        removes a lock.
    -->
    <string name="locks_unavailable">%1$s is no longer installed. Its lock stays until you remove it.</string>

    <!--
        Removal (005 US5, FR-021, FR-022, contract K4).

        SlowLock CANNOT remove a lock. A lock is its pinned shortcut, Android offers no way to
        unpin one, and the only place the action exists is the user's home screen. So this is not
        a confirmation - there is nothing to confirm - it is an explanation, with one button.

        This is the label of the long press and of the custom accessibility action that opens it.
        It is phrased as a question rather than a command because choosing it performs nothing.
    -->
    <string name="locks_removal_action">How to remove this lock</string>

    <!--
        The visible control on an unavailable row (contract K3), which has no tap target for a long
        press to attach to. Shorter than the action label because it sits inside a row.
    -->
    <string name="locks_removal_help">How to remove</string>

    <string name="locks_removal_title">Removing a lock</string>

    <!--
        THE WORDING IS THE DELIVERABLE (FR-022, SC-012, Constitution I). Read the constraints before
        touching or translating this string:

        - It MUST name the app (%1$s), so the user can tell which icon to look for.
        - It MUST say that removing the icon is what removes the lock, and that the user does it on
          their home screen. This is the whole content of the dialog.
        - It MUST NOT imply SlowLock can remove the icon, or that anything happens when this dialog
          is dismissed. Nothing does - there is one button and it closes the dialog.
        - It MUST NOT suggest the app is uninstalled. It is not, and the last sentence says so
          because "remove" next to an app name reads as "uninstall" to plenty of people.
    -->
    <string name="locks_removal_body">The lock for %1$s disappears from this list when you remove its icon from your home screen.\n\nPress and hold the icon, then drag it away or choose Remove. This does not uninstall the app.</string>

    <string name="locks_removal_dismiss">OK</string>
    <!--
        The wizard's step counter (FR-029, contract K5, research R7).

        The "3" is a literal in the resource, never a computed stage count: the flow has three
        steps because the design says it has three, not because the app happens to hold three
        stages, and a translator moving the separator must be able to see both numbers.
        %1$d is the current step. Mono, so the two numerals do not shift width between steps.
    -->
    <string name="step_counter">%1$d / 3</string>

    <!--
        What the counter is announced as, merged into the header's title so a screen reader hears
        "Choose an app, step 1 of 3" rather than the title and a bare "1 / 3" as two separate
        stops. The visible form above is glanceable; this one is a sentence, which is why the two
        cannot be the same resource. It is not a control and has no action.
    -->
    <string name="step_counter_description">step %1$d of 3</string>

</resources>
